<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Malplaquet</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">11 September <a href="/years/1709">1709</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · <a href="/today-in-history/september-11">On this day: 11 September</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Malplaquet was fought on 11 September 1709 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mons">Mons</a>, in Hainaut, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a>. An allied army under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-churchill-1st-duke-of-marlborough">Duke of Marlborough</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-eugene-of-savoy">Prince Eugene of Savoy</a> attacked the French under Marshal Villars and forced them to withdraw, but at such cost that it was the bloodiest battle of the war and brought peace no closer.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-malplaquet.jpg" alt="Battle of Malplaquet" width="320" height="253" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Copy_after_Jan_van_Huchtenburgh_(Haarlem_1647-Amsterdam_1733)_-_The_Battle_of_Malplaquet,_1709_-_RCIN_404898_-_Royal_Collection.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its southern towns fell to Louis XIV, and the battles of <b>Malplaquet</b> in 1709 and Jemappes in 1792, where the French revolutionary army defeated the Austrians near Mons, were fought on Hainaut soil.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hainaut">County of Hainaut · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the War of the Spanish Succession John Churchill, Duke of Marlborough, won at Ramillies in 1706, Oudenarde in 1708 and <b>Malplaquet</b> in 1709, and the Treaty of Utrecht (1713) gave Britain Gibraltar, Minorca, Newfoundland and Nova Scotia and the asiento to supply enslaved Africans to Spanish America.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>It is a burlesque lament on the death of John Churchill, 1st Duke of Marlborough (1650–1722), written on a false rumour of his death after the <b>Battle of Malplaquet</b> in 1709, the bloodiest battle of the War of the Spanish Succession.</blockquote><a href="/songs/malbrough-sen-va-t-en-guerre">Malbrough s'en va-t-en guerre</a></li></ul></section>
